WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.713
You're in a city 10,000
fathoms beneath the sea.

00:00:04.713 --> 00:00:06.756
It's quite beautiful.

00:00:06.756 --> 00:00:08.091
Living under the sea

00:00:08.091 --> 00:00:09.759
isn't just Hollywood fantasy.

00:00:09.759 --> 00:00:11.261
Requesting landing clearance, over.

00:00:11.261 --> 00:00:14.264
Can we build an underwater city? Sure.

00:00:14.264 --> 00:00:15.098
Whether we build them

00:00:15.098 --> 00:00:19.102
to survive overpopulation or
catastrophic climate change,

00:00:19.102 --> 00:00:21.980
or to help scientists learn
more about our planet.

00:00:23.314 --> 00:00:24.190
It stands to reason

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:26.234
that we must push the boundaries

00:00:26.234 --> 00:00:30.071
of ocean exploration so that
we can bring back solutions.

00:00:31.448 --> 00:00:33.825
The deep sea could teach
us about our own origins

00:00:33.825 --> 00:00:36.578
and if we can learn about
how life originated here,

00:00:36.578 --> 00:00:37.579
we might be able to learn something

00:00:37.579 --> 00:00:40.665
about how life may have
originated elsewhere.

00:00:41.124 --> 00:00:42.333
We already know more

00:00:42.333 --> 00:00:45.712
about the surface of the moon
than the depths of our oceans.

00:00:46.337 --> 00:00:47.630
In comparison to going to the moon

00:00:47.630 --> 00:00:50.467
living underwater is not that complicated.

00:00:57.974 --> 00:01:00.143
While our relationship
with the ocean started

00:01:00.143 --> 00:01:02.062
tens of thousands of years ago,

00:01:02.062 --> 00:01:05.190
the technology that enabled
humans to explore underwater

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:07.317
is relatively recent.

00:01:07.317 --> 00:01:11.071
In 1943, a French Naval
officer called Jacques Cousteau

00:01:11.071 --> 00:01:15.325
invented the Aqualung,
the precursor to scuba.

00:01:15.325 --> 00:01:18.661
In his 1964 film "World Without Sun"

00:01:18.661 --> 00:01:21.498
he explored the sea in
his two-man submarine,

00:01:21.498 --> 00:01:23.500
the Diving Saucer.

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:24.584
He'd park it in a garage,

00:01:24.584 --> 00:01:27.712
which was part of his subaquatic village.

00:01:27.712 --> 00:01:30.006
These men are the first oceanauts.

00:01:31.674 --> 00:01:33.927
They are going to live
down here for a month.

00:01:34.969 --> 00:01:37.972
His team spent 30 days beneath the waves.

00:01:39.099 --> 00:01:41.935
These sunken houses permit us to escape

00:01:41.935 --> 00:01:43.812
many limitations of diving.

00:01:44.729 --> 00:01:46.231
More habitats followed,

00:01:46.231 --> 00:01:49.317
built by amateur enthusiasts
as well as serious players,

00:01:49.317 --> 00:01:52.153
such as the U.S. Navy and NASA.

00:01:52.153 --> 00:01:54.906
The sea was the next
frontier to be explored,

00:01:54.906 --> 00:01:58.076
and there was interest from
beyond the scientific community.

00:01:58.076 --> 00:02:02.080
President Kennedy wrote to
the U.S. Senate in 1961:

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:05.917
Knowledge of the oceans is
more than a matter of curiosity.

00:02:05.917 --> 00:02:08.336
Our very survival may hinge on it.

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:14.551
In the 1950s and '60s,
that particular time period,

00:02:14.551 --> 00:02:16.970
there's a historical context there

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:21.015
that makes underwater living
particularly desirable.

00:02:21.015 --> 00:02:23.560
So there are military applications,

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:26.646
and this is the birth of
the offshore oil industry

00:02:26.646 --> 00:02:29.023
and various underwater
construction projects.

00:02:29.023 --> 00:02:31.109
And then you also have a third reason,

00:02:31.109 --> 00:02:32.986
which has perhaps less of an incentive,

00:02:32.986 --> 00:02:36.239
and that's that marine biology
is starting to develop.

00:02:36.239 --> 00:02:37.699
What indeed are the limits

00:02:37.699 --> 00:02:40.785
on flesh and blood beneath the waves?

00:02:40.869 --> 00:02:43.037
Were people looking at
the physiological effects

00:02:43.037 --> 00:02:46.583
on the body, as opposed
to say the scientific work

00:02:46.583 --> 00:02:48.543
that could be done from those depths?

00:02:48.543 --> 00:02:51.337
So one of the problems
in underwater habitats

00:02:51.337 --> 00:02:54.591
is that you have to change the gas mixture

00:02:54.591 --> 00:02:56.676
that can be breathed safely at depth.

00:02:56.676 --> 00:02:59.637
If you just breathe normal
air that we breathe here,

00:02:59.637 --> 00:03:01.055
or if you breathe pure oxygen,

00:03:01.055 --> 00:03:03.892
it becomes toxic or you
get nitrogen narcosis.

00:03:03.892 --> 00:03:06.644
So they had to combine
different types of gas

00:03:06.644 --> 00:03:08.980
to breathe at pressure,

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:10.982
and they were breathing helium mixtures

00:03:10.982 --> 00:03:12.066
and nitrogen mixtures,

00:03:12.066 --> 00:03:14.068
which is why when you watch
videos with these aquanauts,

00:03:14.068 --> 00:03:16.070
they always have that high helium voice.

00:03:16.404 --> 00:03:17.322
Ca va?

00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:21.409
The seas remain largely unexplored

00:03:21.409 --> 00:03:23.661
because of the limits of the human body.

00:03:23.661 --> 00:03:25.997
We can only dive for a few hours.

00:03:25.997 --> 00:03:28.249
Every breath of compressed air at depth

00:03:28.249 --> 00:03:30.710
requires decompression after,

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:32.003
be it in a chamber,

00:03:32.003 --> 00:03:34.923
or by slowly rising
back up to the surface.

00:03:34.923 --> 00:03:38.009
Without it, divers can develop
decompression sickness,

00:03:38.009 --> 00:03:40.887
also known as the bends, where
nitrogen in your bloodstream

00:03:40.887 --> 00:03:43.514
begins to bubble, damaging blood vessels

00:03:43.514 --> 00:03:45.475
and blocking blood flow.

00:03:45.475 --> 00:03:46.976
It's potentially lethal.

00:03:48.394 --> 00:03:50.396
So living in an underwater habitat,

00:03:50.396 --> 00:03:53.983
like Cousteau's aquanauts,
the body stays saturated

00:03:53.983 --> 00:03:56.819
with inert gases, enabling them to dive

00:03:56.819 --> 00:03:59.989
and work without decompression.

00:04:00.448 --> 00:04:01.699
If man need not return

00:04:01.699 --> 00:04:03.117
to the surface periodically,

00:04:03.117 --> 00:04:04.911
taking a long time to decompress

00:04:04.911 --> 00:04:07.664
those potentially fatal
bubbles within his body,

00:04:07.664 --> 00:04:10.458
he can be infinitely more
productive down below.

00:04:11.459 --> 00:04:13.878
But one of the problems is
that as soon as you have

00:04:13.878 --> 00:04:16.923
your breathing, you're living
in a helium atmosphere,

00:04:16.923 --> 00:04:20.802
it is not very good for
temperature control at all,

00:04:20.802 --> 00:04:22.679
and if you're living in the ocean,

00:04:22.679 --> 00:04:23.846
you can imagine it's quite cold.

00:04:23.846 --> 00:04:26.933
So one of the problems
that they had to figure out

00:04:26.933 --> 00:04:29.894
how to adapt to is that the
divers are constantly cold,

00:04:29.894 --> 00:04:33.022
they're freezing in
this helium atmosphere.

00:04:33.022 --> 00:04:35.233
And then the other problem
is that once you're staying

00:04:35.233 --> 00:04:36.818
at depth for prolonged periods,

00:04:36.818 --> 00:04:38.528
so unlike a submarine where you can go up,

00:04:38.528 --> 00:04:41.072
purge the air, and go back down,

00:04:41.072 --> 00:04:44.909
you have to develop some sort
of air circulation system

00:04:44.909 --> 00:04:47.704
that is purging CO2 constantly.

00:04:49.038 --> 00:04:51.165
And that only really works well

00:04:51.165 --> 00:04:53.668
if you can keep the humidity down.

00:04:53.668 --> 00:04:56.379
If you're living in a metal
container under the ocean,

00:04:56.379 --> 00:04:58.464
it's difficult to keep the humidity down.

00:04:58.464 --> 00:05:01.509
So controlling all of these
atmospheric conditions

00:05:01.509 --> 00:05:04.387
was one of the really big hurdles

00:05:04.387 --> 00:05:06.723
for making it safe for
people to live at depth.

00:05:06.723 --> 00:05:08.182
A lot of these habitats came and went

00:05:08.182 --> 00:05:09.559
in the '60s and '70s.

00:05:09.559 --> 00:05:12.437
Can you give us some sense
of the historical context?

00:05:12.437 --> 00:05:14.314
In the 1960s and the 1970s,

00:05:14.314 --> 00:05:17.525
this is when the space race is going on.

00:05:17.525 --> 00:05:20.695
So there was this analog
between the exploration

00:05:20.695 --> 00:05:24.198
of inner space, the
oceans and outer space

00:05:24.198 --> 00:05:31.122
and already in the U.S.,
people in government,

00:05:31.122 --> 00:05:33.499
at NASA and in the Navy were looking ahead

00:05:33.499 --> 00:05:36.085
to future space exploration missions

00:05:36.085 --> 00:05:38.046
and thinking that here underwater,

00:05:38.046 --> 00:05:39.756
we have a perfect analog example

00:05:39.756 --> 00:05:43.426
where we can already study
how crews are gonna behave

00:05:43.426 --> 00:05:45.678
in isolation in a small space,

00:05:45.678 --> 00:05:48.348
over long periods of time.

00:05:48.348 --> 00:05:50.558
So the Cold War is a
really important context

00:05:50.558 --> 00:05:52.268
for understanding this as well.

00:05:52.268 --> 00:05:54.395
And the Russians were going into space

00:05:54.395 --> 00:05:56.439
and there were a few Soviet habitats

00:05:56.439 --> 00:06:00.568
but they never seemed like a viable,

00:06:00.568 --> 00:06:03.321
real competition to the Americans.

00:06:03.321 --> 00:06:05.615
So underwater competition
between the Americans

00:06:05.615 --> 00:06:09.369
and the Soviets played out
with nuclear submarines

00:06:09.369 --> 00:06:10.995
but not underwater habitats.

00:06:10.995 --> 00:06:13.247
Nobody was colonizing the sea floor.

00:06:13.247 --> 00:06:16.334
I think the technology was
proven in the 1960s and '70s

00:06:16.334 --> 00:06:20.088
but there were other reasons
that it wasn't that useful

00:06:20.088 --> 00:06:23.800
or proved to not be something
that was necessary to do

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:25.093
for a long period of time.

00:06:25.093 --> 00:06:27.387
The technology developed
that allowed divers

00:06:27.387 --> 00:06:31.974
to compress on shipboard
instead, instead of at depth.

00:06:31.974 --> 00:06:34.268
It will take two and one half hours

00:06:34.268 --> 00:06:36.437
for their bodies to be
equal to the same pressure

00:06:36.437 --> 00:06:38.481
they will experience on the ocean floor.

00:06:38.481 --> 00:06:41.692
So you could be compressed
in a compression chamber

00:06:41.692 --> 00:06:44.529
in a ship, then lowered in a capsule

00:06:44.529 --> 00:06:46.197
while still under pressure,

00:06:46.197 --> 00:06:48.950
exit the capsule, work
on the oil head rig,

00:06:48.950 --> 00:06:51.244
get back in the capsule
and remain pressurized

00:06:51.244 --> 00:06:52.620
at the surface.

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:54.914
And that technology did develop

00:06:54.914 --> 00:06:58.292
out of all of these experiments
with underwater habitats

00:06:58.292 --> 00:07:00.211
but it sort of took
the underwater habitats

00:07:00.211 --> 00:07:01.379
out of the equation.

00:07:01.379 --> 00:07:04.882
Scientists still see value in
spending time under the sea.

00:07:04.882 --> 00:07:07.593
What sort of questions
are they trying to answer?

00:07:07.593 --> 00:07:09.887
My wife is a marine microbiologist,

00:07:09.887 --> 00:07:11.222
who's actually here if you want

00:07:11.222 --> 00:07:12.849
to ask her questions about this.

00:07:12.849 --> 00:07:13.724
Yeah, that'd be great.

00:07:13.724 --> 00:07:14.600
All right.

00:07:17.186 --> 00:07:18.312
Hi.

00:07:18.312 --> 00:07:19.105
Hi.

00:07:19.105 --> 00:07:21.149
I am a marine microbiologist,

00:07:21.149 --> 00:07:23.734
mostly focusing on hydrothermal vents,

00:07:23.734 --> 00:07:25.027
and I've been doing that for a while now.

00:07:25.027 --> 00:07:27.405
And my advisor, my PhD advisor was the one

00:07:27.405 --> 00:07:28.990
who first suggested
that the origin of life

00:07:28.990 --> 00:07:30.533
may have been at hydrothermal events.

00:07:30.533 --> 00:07:32.952
Yeah. That's one of humanity's
big questions, I guess.

00:07:32.952 --> 00:07:34.996
So, you know, do you think those answers

00:07:34.996 --> 00:07:37.039
could be at the bottom of the sea?

00:07:37.039 --> 00:07:38.916
We like to think so.

00:07:38.916 --> 00:07:41.377
The original idea basically
was that hydrothermal vents

00:07:41.377 --> 00:07:42.962
were kind of like a natural laboratory

00:07:42.962 --> 00:07:44.714
because they're dominated by gradients.

00:07:44.714 --> 00:07:47.300
So in the middle of a
chimney, it's really hot,

00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:49.802
it's water that can be up
to 400 degrees Celsius,

00:07:49.802 --> 00:07:50.761
and then two feet away,

00:07:50.761 --> 00:07:52.805
the water's like two
degrees Celsius, right?

00:07:52.805 --> 00:07:55.099
So in between you have every temperature

00:07:55.099 --> 00:07:57.810
you could possibly want
for all kinds of reactions.

00:07:57.810 --> 00:07:59.228
They're very hard to study

00:07:59.228 --> 00:08:00.813
because they're at the
bottom of the ocean,

00:08:00.813 --> 00:08:02.190
thousands of meters deep,

00:08:02.190 --> 00:08:04.275
and we can only really take samples

00:08:04.275 --> 00:08:05.776
when we can get a ship out there.

00:08:05.776 --> 00:08:08.738
If you had the opportunity to spend,

00:08:08.738 --> 00:08:12.408
say a month underwater
next to one of these vents,

00:08:12.408 --> 00:08:15.661
from a scientific angle,
would that be helpful?

00:08:15.661 --> 00:08:17.371
Yeah, so there's, there's
two answers there.

00:08:17.371 --> 00:08:19.916
One is we do use submarines,
so I have been there.

00:08:19.916 --> 00:08:22.251
I've been in Alvin down to these vents

00:08:22.251 --> 00:08:24.295
and being there in person is amazing

00:08:24.295 --> 00:08:26.088
because you get a much better sense

00:08:26.088 --> 00:08:27.590
of what the environment looks like

00:08:27.590 --> 00:08:29.050
in a three-dimensional way.

00:08:29.050 --> 00:08:32.261
Just being in that space I
just felt like I had a much

00:08:32.261 --> 00:08:34.555
better sense of what these
habitats really look like

00:08:34.555 --> 00:08:36.682
and how connected they
are and whether microbes

00:08:36.682 --> 00:08:39.769
and ingredients could flow
from one vent to the next.

00:08:39.769 --> 00:08:41.187
The thing that I would really value

00:08:41.187 --> 00:08:43.731
whether I was present or
not is just being able

00:08:43.731 --> 00:08:46.692
to look at these things
longitudinally over time.

00:08:46.692 --> 00:08:49.362
Understanding how the
microbes adapt over time

00:08:49.362 --> 00:08:51.489
is something we really
haven't addressed yet

00:08:51.489 --> 00:08:53.366
and is something I'm working on right now

00:08:53.366 --> 00:08:54.825
to try to understand better.

00:08:54.825 --> 00:08:56.827
So I think there's a lot of value in that.

00:08:56.827 --> 00:08:58.621
Now, whether I would need
to be physically present

00:08:58.621 --> 00:09:00.665
for that period of time
is another question,

00:09:00.665 --> 00:09:01.999
because I like bathrooms

00:09:01.999 --> 00:09:05.002
and I like having food and
sunlight, so maybe not.

00:09:05.002 --> 00:09:08.297
We enjoy the adventure of going
down there, but in the end,

00:09:08.297 --> 00:09:11.133
we do want to sleep in a soft
bed that's not cold and damp

00:09:11.133 --> 00:09:12.218
at the end of the day.

00:09:13.386 --> 00:09:15.972
The underwater habitats
of the 20th century

00:09:15.972 --> 00:09:17.974
have mostly been retired.

00:09:18.391 --> 00:09:20.935
A couple still remain serving scientists,

00:09:20.935 --> 00:09:23.062
but a focus on space exploration

00:09:23.062 --> 00:09:25.898
and the rise of robotic
technology has decreased the need

00:09:25.898 --> 00:09:28.818
for humans to live beneath the waves.

00:09:29.277 --> 00:09:31.487
But could there be a resurgence?

00:09:31.487 --> 00:09:35.116
Fabien Cousteau, grandson
of Jacques and an aquanaut,

00:09:35.116 --> 00:09:38.035
explorer and conservationist
in his own right,

00:09:38.035 --> 00:09:41.747
spent 31 days underwater in 2014.

00:09:41.747 --> 00:09:44.667
Can you give me a sense of the science

00:09:44.667 --> 00:09:47.169
that can be achieved
when you're underwater

00:09:47.169 --> 00:09:49.755
for extended periods of time?

00:09:49.755 --> 00:09:52.466
My team and I were able to execute

00:09:52.466 --> 00:09:55.219
over three years' worth
of equivalent science,

00:09:55.219 --> 00:09:59.599
as opposed to being
based from a ship above.

00:09:59.599 --> 00:10:02.059
And that has a lot to do with the access

00:10:02.059 --> 00:10:03.185
to the bottom world.

00:10:04.312 --> 00:10:08.274
By being able to be based
out of an underwater habitat,

00:10:08.274 --> 00:10:11.902
it's a very unique platform,
it's a very unique tool.

00:10:11.902 --> 00:10:14.113
In the case of being saturated,

00:10:14.113 --> 00:10:17.992
we could dive 10 to 12
hours, each of us, per day

00:10:17.992 --> 00:10:22.455
to take advantage of
that and to be able to do

00:10:22.455 --> 00:10:25.708
much, much more research
in a workday than you can

00:10:25.708 --> 00:10:27.335
from the surface.

00:10:27.335 --> 00:10:28.836
Additionally, as a human being,

00:10:28.836 --> 00:10:32.798
being right on the ocean's final frontier,

00:10:33.674 --> 00:10:37.845
you're able to do things
that simply are not

00:10:37.845 --> 00:10:42.850
as easily accessible from an
ROV or an AUV or a submersible,

00:10:44.185 --> 00:10:48.105
but there's still a human factor
that needs to be integrated

00:10:48.105 --> 00:10:50.358
into these kinds of exercises.

00:10:50.358 --> 00:10:52.068
And with Mission 31,

00:10:52.068 --> 00:10:58.074
we were able to use all
sorts of laboratory equipment

00:10:58.074 --> 00:11:01.118
that normally are relegated
to a laboratory on land

00:11:01.118 --> 00:11:03.412
and bring them back down
to the bottom of the sea.

00:11:10.169 --> 00:11:11.629
Fabien, you're raising money

00:11:11.629 --> 00:11:14.674
for a new underwater
habitat called Proteus.

00:11:14.674 --> 00:11:16.425
How's it different from the ones before

00:11:16.425 --> 00:11:18.678
and why do we need it now?

00:11:19.970 --> 00:11:23.391
With Proteus, we're talking about a space

00:11:23.391 --> 00:11:27.269
that is about ten times
the size of Aquarius

00:11:27.269 --> 00:11:31.315
and a team that will be twice the size

00:11:31.315 --> 00:11:34.735
of any previous team deployed underwater,

00:11:34.735 --> 00:11:37.530
so about a dozen people.

00:11:37.530 --> 00:11:41.283
And with that, we'll be
testing the physiological

00:11:41.283 --> 00:11:44.787
and psychological parameters
within which it's conducive

00:11:44.787 --> 00:11:48.207
to deploy people for long
periods of time underwater,

00:11:48.207 --> 00:11:52.294
not days or weeks, which has
been done many times before,

00:11:52.294 --> 00:11:55.297
but weeks, months, and maybe even longer.

00:11:55.297 --> 00:11:58.509
I anticipate a platform like Proteus

00:11:58.509 --> 00:12:00.428
being able to offer us a lot more

00:12:00.428 --> 00:12:04.432
of that advanced technology
onsite on the final frontier

00:12:04.432 --> 00:12:05.516
at the bottom of the sea,

00:12:05.516 --> 00:12:09.729
so that our team can do all
of that research right there

00:12:09.729 --> 00:12:11.105
in real time.

00:12:11.105 --> 00:12:14.650
And in the case of things
like viral pandemics,

00:12:14.650 --> 00:12:17.069
to be able to take those
chemical compositions

00:12:17.069 --> 00:12:21.574
of those sets of samples
right there on site

00:12:21.574 --> 00:12:24.368
and analyze them and
hopefully replicate them

00:12:24.368 --> 00:12:27.830
to accelerate that research process,

00:12:27.830 --> 00:12:30.916
rather than having to send
that sample to the surface,

00:12:30.916 --> 00:12:32.877
have it potentially get corrupted

00:12:32.877 --> 00:12:36.213
and get sent halfway around
the world to sit in a freezer

00:12:36.213 --> 00:12:38.841
or a locker for months, if not years,

00:12:38.841 --> 00:12:40.593
before it gets analyzed.

00:12:44.388 --> 00:12:47.183
There's a small group of
crazy people like myself

00:12:47.183 --> 00:12:48.517
that really believe in this

00:12:48.517 --> 00:12:51.645
and I think that the
scientific community as well

00:12:51.645 --> 00:12:52.563
is really excited.

